target: internal/push/push.go
verify: go test ./internal/push/ -count=1 -run 'TestCheckDiagnosesARunMissingFromTheStore'
expect: it says "refusal" on a push it just permitted
why:    the exact wording this note SHIPPED with, for one cycle. The empty record grants as readily as it refuses — `unknown` is a disclosure, not a block, and the collapsed `0 cycle(s) · 0 agent(s)` fingerprint is what pr-report.py renders for a run with no cycle rows, 22 of the live store's 41 — and the note is written before Decide, so on the grant path it told the operator to go read a refusal that was not there. It shipped that way because all four of its original subtests were refusals. An earlier version of this entry tried to express the defect by inserting `if !ValidRunID(p.RunID)`, which is a NO-OP: Check validates the id before this point, so the branch is dead and the mutation survived a 177-entry catalog. The wording is the defect, so the wording is the mutation.
--- old
			fmt.Fprintf(p.Diag, "push-check: no readable row for run %q in %q, so convergence, "+
				"the outcome and the required report fingerprint are all derived from an empty "+
				"record; the decision below rests on nothing the record said\n",
				p.RunID, p.Store)
--- new
			fmt.Fprintf(p.Diag, "push-check: no readable row for run %q in %q, so convergence, "+
				"the outcome and the required report fingerprint are all derived from an empty "+
				"record; the refusal below may not be about the report at all\n",
				p.RunID, p.Store)
